From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ahnaf Shahriar <ahnaf.shahriar@neuralink.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Mar 2026 00:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] tlib: ARM-M WFI should wake on any pending enabled IRQ
 regardless of priority

Per ARM DDI0403E B1.4.3, WFI wakes on any pending exception that is
enabled, regardless of whether it can preempt the current execution
priority (BASEPRI, PRIMASK, active exception priority).

The existing cpu_has_work() used tlib_nvic_get_pending_masked_irq()
for the WFI wake check.  This function returns true only when a
pending interrupt can preempt the current context (passes priority
comparison in FindPendingInterrupt).  When the CPU is inside an
SVCall handler and a lower-priority timer IRQ fires, the IRQ is
pending+enabled but cannot preempt SVCall, so WFI never wakes.

On real hardware this works because WFI wakes on ANY pending enabled
exception, even if it can't be taken immediately.  The CPU wakes,
executes the next few instructions (typically a loop check), and the
pending IRQ fires later when the handler returns and priority allows.

This matters for the nRF52840 SoftDevice which calls sd_app_evt_wait()
-> SVC handler -> WFE/WFI.  Inside the SVCall handler, RTC1 (priority
6) and TIMER1 (priority 4) interrupts are pending but can't preempt
SVCall (priority 4 or lower).  Without this fix the CPU stays in WFI
forever, causing the main loop to stall and the WDT to fire.
---
 arch/arm/arch_callbacks.c | 2 ++
 arch/arm/arch_callbacks.h | 1 +
 arch/arm/cpu.h            | 7 ++++++-
 3 files changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm/arch_callbacks.c b/arch/arm/arch_callbacks.c
index c8b38759..9ec4c4f9 100644
--- a/arch/arm/arch_callbacks.c
+++ b/arch/arm/arch_callbacks.c
@@ -36,6 +36,8 @@ DEFAULT_INT_HANDLER1(int32_t tlib_nvic_find_pending_irq, void)
 
 DEFAULT_INT_HANDLER1(int32_t tlib_nvic_get_pending_masked_irq, void)
 
+DEFAULT_INT_HANDLER1(int32_t tlib_nvic_any_pending_enabled_irq, void)
+
 DEFAULT_VOID_HANDLER1(void tlib_nvic_set_pending_irq, int32_t number)
 
 DEFAULT_INT_HANDLER1(uint32_t tlib_has_enabled_trustzone, void)
diff --git a/arch/arm/arch_callbacks.h b/arch/arm/arch_callbacks.h
index f78c890a..883073db 100644
--- a/arch/arm/arch_callbacks.h
+++ b/arch/arm/arch_callbacks.h
@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@ void tlib_nvic_complete_irq(int32_t number);
 void tlib_nvic_write_basepri(int32_t number, uint32_t secure);
 int32_t tlib_nvic_find_pending_irq(void);
 int32_t tlib_nvic_get_pending_masked_irq(void);
+int32_t tlib_nvic_any_pending_enabled_irq(void);
 void tlib_nvic_set_pending_irq(int32_t no);
 uint32_t tlib_has_enabled_trustzone(void);
 uint32_t tlib_nvic_interrupt_targets_secure(int32_t no);
diff --git a/arch/arm/cpu.h b/arch/arm/cpu.h
index e27e00fd..be541d93 100644
--- a/arch/arm/cpu.h
+++ b/arch/arm/cpu.h
@@ -931,7 +931,12 @@ static inline bool cpu_has_work(CPUState *env)
 #ifndef TARGET_PROTO_ARM_M
         has_work = is_interrupt_pending(env, CPU_INTERRUPT_FIQ | CPU_INTERRUPT_HARD | CPU_INTERRUPT_EXITTB);
 #else
-        has_work = tlib_nvic_get_pending_masked_irq() != 0;
+        // ARM DDI0403E B1.4.3: WFI wakes on any pending enabled
+        // exception, regardless of priority or masking (BASEPRI,
+        // PRIMASK).  Use the priority-independent check here so the
+        // CPU wakes even when inside a handler that masks lower-
+        // priority interrupts.
+        has_work = tlib_nvic_any_pending_enabled_irq() != 0;
 #endif
         if(has_work) {
             env->wfi = 0;
